// The section "Iterators" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Iterators() {
    return <>
        <p>
            An iterator is a function whose one parameter is <code>yield</code>, as in Go 1.23:
            {" "}<code>{"func(yield func(number) bool)"}</code>. It calls <code>yield(v)</code> for
            each value, and <code>for v in seq</code> goes over them. When the loop stops, the
            iterator stops at its <code>yield</code> too, as if it had given <code>false</code>,
            and its <code>defer</code> runs. A <code>yield</code> of two values gives a key and a
            value: <code>for place, fruit in numbered(fruits)</code>.
        </p>
        <Example name="iterators" />
        <p>
            An iterator compiles to a JS generator, and <code>yield(v)</code> to
            {" "}<code>yield v</code>. So it is a lazy JS iterator too: <code>seq()</code> gives one,
            with <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>take</code>, and JS code goes over
            it with <code>for...of</code>. For the same reason an iterator calls <code>yield</code>
            {" "}only in its own body, not in a function inside it, and it is not async.
        </p>
    </>
}
